---
Part XIII, Chapter 4: The boundaries of a trope
Package: packages/loom-lang/
Language: TypeScript
---

# The boundaries of a trope

A trope gathers the chapters that serve one purpose, such as the payments of a shop. A program keeps such a cluster whole by drawing a boundary around it. A few chapters are the ways in, which other code may use, and the rest is the inside, which nobody outside touches. A package's `exports` field draws that line for npm, and a module's `exports` clause draws it for Java. A trope draws it in the frontmatter of its chapters.

```
---
Part III, Chapter 4: Payments
Trope: Payments
Entries: Charging, Refunds
---
```

```
---
Part III, Chapter 5: Charging
Rooted in: Payments
---
```

The `Trope:` line makes a chapter the root of the trope `Payments`, and a trope has one root, as the chapter "The kinds tropes declare" describes. A `Rooted in:` line makes a chapter one of the trope's members. The root's `Entries:` line lists the trope's entries, the ways in: the chapters that a chapter outside the trope may reach. The root is an entry only when it lists itself. Otherwise it declares the trope without being a way in, as a package's `package.json` declares the package.

A root with no `Entries:` line is the trope's only entry. The Mermaid trope of Part XIV has such a root, and no members.

## What lies inside

Every chapter of a trope except its entries lies inside the trope. Loom never asks an author to list more than that, and derives the rest from what the chapters already say. A chapter reaches another when an anchor in it names the other, or when a box in it points to the other, as the chapter "The multi-file AST" lists. A trope reaches whatever its root and its entries reach, step by step, through the trope's own chapters.

Three judgements follow from those reaches. A member that neither the root nor an entry reaches is a missing link or dead code, like a file nobody imports, so Loom warns of it. A chapter that the trope reaches and that names no trope belongs to the trope by use alone. Loom marks it to be settled, so a trope never grows past what its author meant without the author seeing it. A chapter outside the trope may reach the trope's entries and nothing else. Loom faults any other reach across the boundary, at the anchor or the box that makes it. A trope box's `anchors` key reaches its trope too, so it must name an entry.

A trope whose chapters are all members has no root yet. Loom draws no boundary around it, and notes that the trope is to be settled. An `Entries:` line belongs to a root alone, so Loom faults one on any other chapter and reads nothing from it.

## A trope's boundary

A `Boundary` holds what Loom derives for one rooted trope: the trope's name, its `root`, the `entries` its names lead to, and the `unknown` names, which lead to no chapter the trope may make an entry. `guarded` holds the trope's chapters that are not entries, which no chapter outside the trope may reach. `unreached` holds the members nothing reaches, and `unsettled` the chapters the trope reaches by use alone. `insiders` holds the paths of every chapter that belongs to the trope, its own chapters and the chapters it reaches by use, since those may reach the guarded chapters freely.

An `UnknownName` is a name on the `Entries:` line that Loom cannot make an entry, with its `owner`, the trope that holds the chapter it leads to, when another trope does. `Boundaries` holds the boundary of every rooted trope, and every trope with no root yet.

=>

export type UnknownName = {
  readonly name: FrontmatterEntryToken
  readonly owner: Option.Option<string>
}

export type Boundary = {
  readonly trope: string
  readonly root: LoomModule
  readonly entries: ReadonlyArray<LoomModule>
  readonly unknown: ReadonlyArray<UnknownName>
  readonly guarded: ReadonlyArray<LoomModule>
  readonly unreached: ReadonlyArray<LoomModule>
  readonly unsettled: ReadonlyArray<LoomModule>
  readonly insiders: ReadonlySet<Location>
}

export type Boundaries = {
  readonly bounded: ReadonlyArray<Boundary>
  readonly unrooted: ReadonlyArray<Trope>
}

## Reading the entries

`entryOf` reads one name of the root's `Entries:` line, through the corpus's own `tropeEntryModuleOf`, which resolves the name to a chapter as an anchor's name resolves. A name that leads to no one chapter is unknown. So is a name that leads to a chapter of another trope, since only that trope can make it an entry. A chapter that names no trope may stand as an entry. The trope then reaches it, and Loom marks it to be settled like any chapter the trope reaches by use.

`entriesOf` reads the whole line and separates the chapters it makes entries from the names it cannot. A root with no `Entries:` line is the trope's only chapter, so it is the trope's only entry.

=>

const entryOf = (
  book: Book,
  trope: string,
  name: FrontmatterEntryToken,
): Result.Result<LoomModule, UnknownName> =>
  Option.match(tropeEntryModuleOf(book, name.value), {
    onNone: () => Result.fail({ name, owner: Option.none() }),
    onSome: (module) =>
      Option.match(
        Option.filter(tropeOf(module), (owner) => owner !== trope),
        {
          onNone: () => Result.succeed(module),
          onSome: (owner) => Result.fail({ name, owner: Option.some(owner) }),
        },
      ),
  })

const entriesOf = (
  book: Book,
  trope: string,
  root: LoomModule,
): readonly [ReadonlyArray<LoomModule>, ReadonlyArray<UnknownName>] =>
  Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(root.doc.frontmatter?.entries), {
    onNone: () => [[root], []],
    onSome: (names) => Array.separate(Array.map(names, (name) => entryOf(book, trope, name))),
  })

## Walking what the trope reaches

`boundaryOf` derives one trope's boundary. It walks from the root and the entries with the corpus's own `reach`, along the reaches that `reachesOf` lists. The walk records a chapter outside the trope but never steps out of it, so the walk ends at the trope's edge. When that chapter belongs to another trope, the other trope's own boundary judges the reach. Every member the walk misses is unreached. A second chapter that names the trope with a `Trope:` line is no member, since the registry already refuses it as a second root. Every chapter it finds that names no trope is unsettled, unless it holds a discussion, which `outsideEveryTrope` sets aside, since a discussion belongs to the book rather than to a trope.

`boundariesOf` derives the boundary of every trope that has a root, and keeps aside every trope that has none.

=>

const outsideEveryTrope = (module: LoomModule): boolean =>
  Option.isNone(tropeOf(module)) && module.doc.frontmatter?.discussion === undefined

const boundaryOf = (
  modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>,
  book: Book,
  trope: Trope,
  root: LoomModule,
): Boundary => {
  const chapters = new Map(Array.map(trope.chapters, (chapter) => [chapter.path, chapter] as const))
  const [entries, unknown] = entriesOf(book, trope.name, root)
  const reached = reach([root.path, ...Array.map(entries, (module) => module.path)], (path) =>
    Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(chapters.get(path)), {
      onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<Location> => [],
      onSome: (chapter) => Array.map(reachesOf(book, chapter), (each) => each.target),
    }),
  )
  const unsettled = pipe(
    Array.fromIterable(reached),
    Array.filter((path) => !chapters.has(path)),
    Array.flatMap((path) => Option.toArray(Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(path)))),
    Array.filter(outsideEveryTrope),
  )
  const entered = new Set(Array.map(entries, (module) => module.path))
  return {
    trope: trope.name,
    root,
    entries,
    unknown,
    guarded: Array.filter(trope.chapters, (chapter) => !entered.has(chapter.path)),
    unreached: Array.filter(
      trope.chapters,
      (chapter) => !reached.has(chapter.path) && !rootsTrope(chapter),
    ),
    unsettled,
    insiders: new Set([...chapters.keys(), ...Array.map(unsettled, (module) => module.path)]),
  }
}

export const boundariesOf = (
  modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>,
  book: Book,
  tropes: ReadonlyArray<Trope>,
): Boundaries => ({
  bounded: Array.getSomes(
    Array.map(tropes, (trope) =>
      Option.map(trope.root, (root) => boundaryOf(modules, book, trope, root)),
    ),
  ),
  unrooted: Array.filter(tropes, (trope) => Option.isNone(trope.root)),
})

## Judging a loom against the boundaries

`boundaryFaults` gives a loom the faults that its tropes' boundaries find in it. A member of a trope with no root carries the `UnrootedTrope` note on its `Rooted in:` line, which `tropeLineOf` finds. A root carries the `UnknownEntry` fault on each name of its `Entries:` line that it cannot make an entry, and `entriesOffRoot` gives the `EntriesOffRoot` fault to an `Entries:` line on a chapter with no `Trope:` line, at the line's first name. An unreached member carries the `UnreachedMember` warning on its `Rooted in:` line. A chapter the trope reaches by use alone carries the `UnsettledMember` note on its title, which `titleLineOf` finds, since the frontmatter is where the chapter would name the trope.

=>

const titleLineOf = (module: LoomModule): Position =>
  Option.getOrElse(
    Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter?.title), (title) => title.position),
    () => ({ start: module.doc.position.start, end: module.doc.position.start }),
  )

const isModule =
  (module: LoomModule) =>
  (other: LoomModule): boolean =>
    other.path === module.path

const unrootedFaults = (boundaries: Boundaries, module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> =>
  pipe(
    boundaries.unrooted,
    Array.filter((trope) => Array.some(trope.chapters, isModule(module))),
    Array.flatMap(
      (trope) => faulty(UnrootedTrope({ trope: trope.name }), tropeLineOf(module)).diagnostics,
    ),
  )

const entriesOffRoot = (module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> =>
  rootsTrope(module)
    ? []
    : pipe(
        Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter?.entries),
        Option.flatMap(Array.head),
        Option.match({
          onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> => [],
          onSome: (name) => faulty(EntriesOffRoot(), name.position).diagnostics,
        }),
      )

const entryFaults = (boundary: Boundary, module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> =>
  isModule(module)(boundary.root)
    ? Array.flatMap(
        boundary.unknown,
        ({ name, owner }) =>
          faulty(
            UnknownEntry({
              name: name.value,
              ...Option.match(owner, { onNone: () => ({}), onSome: (held) => ({ owner: held }) }),
            }),
            name.position,
          ).diagnostics,
      )
    : []

const memberFaults = (boundary: Boundary, module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> => [
  ...(Array.some(boundary.unreached, isModule(module))
    ? faulty(UnreachedMember({ trope: boundary.trope }), tropeLineOf(module)).diagnostics
    : []),
  ...(Array.some(boundary.unsettled, isModule(module))
    ? faulty(UnsettledMember({ trope: boundary.trope }), titleLineOf(module)).diagnostics
    : []),
]

export const boundaryFaults = (
  boundaries: Boundaries,
  book: Book,
  module: LoomModule,
): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> => [
  ...unrootedFaults(boundaries, module),
  ...entriesOffRoot(module),
  ...Array.flatMap(boundaries.bounded, (boundary) => [
    ...entryFaults(boundary, module),
    ...memberFaults(boundary, module),
  ]),
  ...reachingInside(boundaries, book, module),
]

~

`reachingInside` judges each reach the loom makes across a boundary. It considers only the tropes that guard a chapter and that the loom does not belong to. A reach that lands on one of their guarded chapters carries the `ReachingInside` fault at its anchor or box, and the fault names the chapter and the entries the trope offers instead. Most books guard nothing, so Loom lists a loom's reaches only when some trope guards a chapter from it.

=>

const reachingInside = (
  boundaries: Boundaries,
  book: Book,
  module: LoomModule,
): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> => {
  const guarding = Array.filter(
    boundaries.bounded,
    (boundary) =>
      Array.isReadonlyArrayNonEmpty(boundary.guarded) && !boundary.insiders.has(module.path),
  )
  return Array.isReadonlyArrayNonEmpty(guarding)
    ? Array.flatMap(reachesOf(book, module), (each) =>
        Array.flatMap(guarding, (boundary) =>
          Option.match(
            Array.findFirst(boundary.guarded, (guarded) => guarded.path === each.target),
            {
              onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> => [],
              onSome: (guarded) =>
                faulty(
                  ReachingInside({
                    trope: boundary.trope,
                    chapter: titleOf(guarded),
                    entries: Array.map(boundary.entries, titleOf),
                  }),
                  each.at,
                ).diagnostics,
            },
          ),
        ),
      )
    : []
}

## What the boundaries draw on

From `effect` come the array, option, and result combinators and `pipe`. From the Loom AST come the `Diagnostic` and `Position` a fault lands with, and the `FrontmatterEntryToken` each name of an `Entries:` line is. The corpus lends the `Book`, the modules, `tropeEntryModuleOf`, which resolves an entry's name, `reachesOf`, which lists a loom's reaches, and `reach`, which walks them. The fault catalog lends `faulty` with the six faults of a boundary. The registry of the chapter "The kinds tropes declare" lends the `Trope` it gathers, `tropeOf` and `tropeLineOf`, which read and find the line a chapter names its trope on, `rootsTrope`, which tells a root, and `titleOf`, which reads a chapter's title.

=>

import { Array, Option, pipe, Result } from 'effect'
import type { Diagnostic, Position } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomNode'
import type { FrontmatterEntryToken } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomTokens'
import {
  type Book,
  tropeEntryModuleOf,
  type Location,
  type LoomModule,
  reach,
  reachesOf,
} from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomCorpusAst'
import {
  EntriesOffRoot,
  faulty,
  ReachingInside,
  UnknownEntry,
  UnreachedMember,
  UnrootedTrope,
  UnsettledMember,
} from './LoomFault'
import { rootsTrope, titleOf, type Trope, tropeLineOf, tropeOf } from './DeclaredKinds'

